Substituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi }^{\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)]}_{-\\pi }^{\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3\\pi ))-\\frac{1}{22}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-11\\pi )) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(0-0)-\\frac{1}{22}(0-0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)$ and $g(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)$ \n$\\text{are orthogonal}$ with respect to the inner product." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560884 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560895 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561049 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560907 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561049 #edb/ref 1069 "q-340637" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561049 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561049 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=3x-4$ for $x\\in R,$ and let $f(x)$ be the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ The Fourier series of $f$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the expression for the coefficient ${a}_{7}$ below, where $⟨\\,,⟩$ denotes the usual inner product on functions over $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{7} & ={{field-1}} \\\\  & ={{field-2}}\\,\\cdot \\,{{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561049 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cf16f70d-fe20-5e04-9e8c-d37751162e97" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561049 #edb/ref 1074 "The usual inner product of functions $f$ and $g$ over $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ is given by\n\n$$\n⟨f(x),g(x)⟩={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)g(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFor a $2\\pi $-periodic function $f(x)$ that is piecewise continuous over $x\\in [0,2\\pi ),$ the Fourier series of $f$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{0},$ ${a}_{n},$ and ${b}_{n}$ are constants to be determined. \n\nNow, we find the inner product of $f(x)$ with $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }({a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx)),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & = \\\\ ⟨\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2},\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }⟨{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & = \\\\ \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}⟨1,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡(nx),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the functions\n$1,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x),\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x),…$\nare orthogonal with respect to the defined inner product, the only nonzero term in the final expression above is\n\n$$\n{a}_{7}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩.\n$$\n\nComputing the inner product, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14x)]}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2\\pi -0)-\\frac{1}{28}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(28\\pi )-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ & ={a}_{7}⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩ \\\\ {a}_{7} & =\\frac{⟨f(x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩}{⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x),\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)⟩} \\\\ {a}_{7} & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(3x-4)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561056 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186560924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561056 #edb/ref 1069 "q-341218" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561056 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561056 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561056 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e89138a9-d6b8-5061-b7fa-8f7c0010f6f0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561056 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f$ is the $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension of $F,$ we have  \n\n- $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $\\pi /3$-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(0+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=f(0)=F(0)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )=-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x)\\ne F(x)$ for $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=0.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f(0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=f(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x)=f(0)=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x+\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\ne f(x)$ for $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561070 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186561084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561070 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "43c884c8-fa1e-5751-bf45-58abee1b87ae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561070 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186560989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561077 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "71d08261-f990-5825-b224-1a3b072f84e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561077 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186315084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561084 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186561090 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561084 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9aa4ea8f-14c6-5c72-8dd1-c9ce0d451eed" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561084 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186561049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561090 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186561077 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561090 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b9b5ba93-4bff-5cc8-b592-e21aafc5699c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561090 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186561056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561094 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186561070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561094 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cce0c6d6-2f7a-5100-bbb3-4f7a6f21298b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186561094 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186167282 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
